        <div id="specs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Makes a Cheap Power Station Good: Rank the Specs, Not the Sticker
          </h2>

          <p>
            A cheap portable power station is not a bad one by default. The budget brands in this guide, OUPES, VTOMAN, Marbero, Pecron and ALLPOWERS, use the same building blocks as the big names: LiFePO4 or lithium-ion cells, a pure sine wave inverter, a battery management system and a set of charging inputs. What changes is the support behind the box, the polish of the app and how carefully the listing describes it. That is why we rank these units on what you get rather than on what they cost.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why This Guide Does Not Quote Prices</h3>

          <p>
            Power station prices on Amazon change daily. Coupons come and go, bundles are repriced, and the same unit can swing a long way within a week. Any figure we printed would be wrong by the time you read it. So every pick here is ranked on capacity, output, battery chemistry, cycle rating, charge time, warranty and ports, and you should check the current Amazon price on the listing before you buy. Compare the price you see against the capacity you get, not against a number in an article.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Capacity in Watt-Hours</h3>

          <p>
            Watt-hours (Wh) are the size of the fuel tank. A 288Wh unit stores roughly a quarter of what a 1024Wh unit does. For budget buyers this is the spec that matters most, because it decides whether the station charges phones for a weekend or keeps a fridge cold overnight. Our guide on <a href="/what-size-portable-power-station-do-i-need/">what size portable power station you need</a> walks through the math.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Continuous and Surge Watts</h3>

          <p>
            Continuous watts are the most the inverter can supply without stopping; surge watts cover the brief spike when a motor starts. A 300W unit runs a laptop, CPAP and fan, but not a kettle. Most small budget units sit between 300W and 600W, which is why they cannot run heating appliances no matter how full the battery is.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cycle Rating and Warranty</h3>

          <p>
            A cycle rating tells you how many full charges the battery is designed to survive before it fades to about 80 percent of its capacity. LiFePO4 units in this guide list 3,000 to 4,000 cycles. The warranty tells you how long the maker will stand behind that claim, and on budget brands it ranges from five years to nothing stated at all, which is one of the clearest differences between them.
          </p>
        </div>

