          </h2>

          <p>
            Searches for a power station "under" a round number are really asking which size class is affordable. Because prices move daily, the useful answer is a capacity class, not a figure. Here is how the budget market breaks down.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Cheapest Class: Under 250Wh</h3>

          <p>
            If you want the lowest-cost unit that still has an AC outlet, look at small 200-250Wh stations like the Marbero M823 and VTOMAN FlashSpeed 300. They charge phones, run lights, a fan and a CPAP for a night or so, and weigh between about 4.5 and 7.5 lbs. Below this class you are mostly looking at large power banks with a small AC outlet, such as Marbero's 88.8Wh model, which suit phones and a laptop rather than real outage use.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Small All-Rounder: 250-300Wh</h3>

          <p>
            Units like the OUPES Exodus 350 add a little capacity, a stronger inverter and faster solar charging. For most people buying their first power station, this is the best value class: light enough to carry, big enough for a weekend of devices.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Mid-Size Step: 450-600Wh</h3>

