        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            14. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best cheap portable power station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people, the OUPES Exodus 350 is the best budget power station: 288Wh of LiFePO4 rated for 3,000+ cycles, a 350W pure sine inverter, 140W USB-C and fast solar charging at 9.2 lbs. If you need more capacity, the Pecron E500LFP doubles it. Prices change daily, so check the current Amazon price.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best budget portable power station for home backup?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Look at 500Wh or larger. The Pecron E500LFP runs a router, CPAP and mini fridge through a short outage. For kitchen appliances or a full-size fridge, the OUPES Mega 1 Lite or ALLPOWERS P1800 offer 1,024Wh of LiFePO4 and 1800-2000W inverters. The VTOMAN FS1100 can expand later.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the cheapest portable power station worth buying?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Look at the smallest class with a pure sine AC outlet, around 230-240Wh, such as the VTOMAN FlashSpeed 300 or Marbero M823. Choose the VTOMAN for a LiFePO4 battery that lasts thousands of cycles, or the Marbero if light weight matters more than battery life. Compare today's prices on both listings.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Pecron as good as Jackery?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On specs, Pecron's E500LFP is competitive: 576Wh of LiFePO4, a 600W pure sine inverter and a listed 1.5-hour AC charge. Jackery generally offers more established support and documentation. Pecron lists a 1+1 year warranty with 24/7 service. Compare the warranty and today's price for the capacity you need.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are cheap power stations safe?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Units from established budget brands with a battery management system and stated protections are generally safe when used within their ratings. Follow the maker's instructions, keep vents clear, avoid overloading and never connect one to house wiring without a proper transfer switch. Avoid unbranded listings with no model number or warranty.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is an affordable solar generator worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A solar generator bundle, such as the ALLPOWERS P1800 with a 100W panel, is a convenient start. Expect small panels to refill large batteries slowly: a single 100W panel takes most of two sunny days to fill a 1kWh station. Add panels up to the station's solar input limit for faster charging.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do cheap power stations last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends mostly on chemistry. LiFePO4 units in this guide list 3,000 to 4,000 cycles to about 80 percent capacity, which is many years of regular use. Lithium-ion units age faster and rarely list a cycle rating. Store either kind partly charged in a cool place to extend its life.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

